CHECK bb02.11 FALSE HYPOTHESIS get.gcv's n argument is sum(w) across weights={all2,int,int10}
CHECK bb02.12 TRUE get.gcv's n argument is nrow(x), unaffected by weights
CHECK bb02.13 FALSE HYPOTHESIS rss.per.subset is the un-normalized weighted RSS sum(w*(y-yhat)^2), for ALL of {all2,int,int10}
CHECK bb02.14 TRUE rss.per.subset is the un-normalized weighted RSS when the weights are not all equal (int, int10)
CHECK bb02.15 TRUE a CONSTANT weight vector (all2) gives a fit identical in every respect to no weights at all
CHECK bb02.16 FALSE HYPOTHESIS rsq's tss is the un-normalized weighted sum(w*(y-weighted.mean(y,w))^2), for ALL of {all2,int,int10}
CHECK bb02.17 TRUE rsq's tss is the un-normalized weighted tss when the weights are not all equal (int, int10)
CHECK bb02.18 FALSE HYPOTHESIS scaling all (non-constant) weights by 10 leaves dirs/cuts/selected.terms unchanged
CHECK bb02.19 TRUE scaling all (non-constant) weights by 10 leaves rsq,grsq unchanged (ratio-invariant, <1e-8 abs diff)
CHECK bb02.20 TRUE scaling all (non-constant) weights by 10 scales raw rss,gcv by 10x (NOT invariant; ratio in [9.99,10.01])
CHECK bb02.21 TRUE gcv.per.subset == rss.per.subset/n for penalty=-1 (maxreldiff=0)
CHECK bb02.22 TRUE gcv.per.subset matches C=nterms rule for penalty=0 (maxreldiff=0)
CHECK bb02.23 TRUE rss == sum(rss.per.response) for the 3-response fit
CHECK bb02.24 TRUE gcv == sum(gcv.per.response) AND gcv.per.response[j] == get.gcv(rss.per.response[j],M,penalty,n)
